mm: page cache reads without folio references (9 commits, tip 4c0f369711)
Source: https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/log/?h=noref-filemap-read-with-llm
Carried unmodified; regenerated with --no-renames.  Base: Merge tag soc-fixes-7.3-2 (0c2669a9f4)

From 5ba7148729528830c7e3caf35766862006988a68 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Linus Torvalds <torvalds@linux-foundation.org>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 02:22:51 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/9] mm: free PG_rcu_free folios only after an RCU grace
 period

A folio with the new PG_rcu_free flag is not returned to the page
allocator when its refcount reaches zero.  __free_pages_prepare() clears
the flag and queues the folio on a per-CPU list, and a work item frees
it with free_unref_folios() once an RCU grace period has elapsed.

A lockless lookup that found the folio under rcu_read_lock() can then
access its memory until rcu_read_unlock(), provided the folio was
removed from where the lookup finds it, and the flag set, before its
last reference was dropped.  The code that removes the folio sets the
flag; the code that drops the last reference needs no change.

The per-CPU state is struct rcu_free_pcpu.  Each CPU keeps at most one
grace period in flight, started with queue_rcu_work().  ->batch holds
the folios it covers, and folios freed meanwhile collect on ->pending.
When the grace period ends, the work takes ->batch, moves ->pending to
->batch, queues the next grace period, and frees what it took.  The
grace period of queue_rcu_work() starts at the call, so the move and the
call are done together under a per-CPU spinlock; otherwise a folio could
join a batch whose grace period started before the folio was freed.
->batch is non-empty from the queue_rcu_work() until the work has taken
it, and a freeing CPU queues only when it is empty.  A folio waits one
to two grace periods.

The two lists are list_heads, and the folios are linked through ->lru.
Both of its words are unused once the refcount has reached zero: the
free that is not deferred links the page through ->pcp_list, which is
the same storage.

A batch holds everything one CPU freed during one grace period, which
for a truncate of a large file can be all of its page cache.  The work
calls cond_resched() after each free_unref_folios(), as the truncate
loops do when they free the folios themselves; without it, on a kernel
without full or lazy preemption, the work would free the whole batch in
one go.

Reclaim makes progress only when these frees complete, so the work must
not wait for a new worker: the workqueue is WQ_MEM_RECLAIM.  It is
allocated from setup_per_cpu_pageset().  start_kernel() calls that
after workqueue_init_early(), from which workqueues can be allocated,
and before vfs_caches_init(), so before anything can be mounted: the
workqueue exists before the first folio is in a page cache.  The
allocation is not checked.

The flag is cleared only when the folio is freed, so a folio that is
still in use can carry it; with the next patch, a shmem folio that went
to the swap cache and was swapped back in does.  That is harmless: its
next free is deferred as well.  Splitting a folio copies the flag to
every new folio, so that no part of a folio is freed earlier than the
whole would have been.

A hwpoisoned order-0 page is not deferred.  __free_pages_prepare() keeps
it out of the allocator in any case, so its memory is not reused, and
page_handle_poison() takes a reference on it right after the put_page()
that brought it there; deferred, the work would later free a page that
has a reference.

The flag must not be set on a folio freed with free_pages_nolock(),
because the deferral takes a spinlock, nor on one freed somewhere other
than the page allocator (hugetlb).

PG_rcu_free takes a page flag bit on every configuration.  32-bit was
not built.

Nothing accounts for folios waiting on the lists: they have refcount 0,
are not PageBuddy and are not in NR_FREE_PAGES.  What that costs once
page cache folios carry the flag is in the next patch.

No user yet.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Linus Torvalds <torvalds@linux-foundation.org>
---
 include/linux/page-flags.h     |  15 ++++
 include/trace/events/mmflags.h |   3 +-
 mm/huge_memory.c               |   1 +
 mm/page_alloc.c                | 126 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 144 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/include/linux/page-flags.h b/include/linux/page-flags.h
index 7a863572a..eedbf35b0 100644
--- a/include/linux/page-flags.h
+++ b/include/linux/page-flags.h
@@ -111,6 +111,7 @@ enum pageflags {
 	PG_swapbacked,		/* Page is backed by RAM/swap */
 	PG_unevictable,		/* Page is "unevictable"  */
 	PG_dropbehind,		/* drop pages on IO completion */
+	PG_rcu_free,		/* free only after an RCU grace period */
 #ifdef CONFIG_MMU
 	PG_mlocked,		/* Page is vma mlocked */
 #endif
@@ -602,6 +603,20 @@ FOLIO_FLAG(dropbehind, FOLIO_HEAD_PAGE)
 	FOLIO_TEST_CLEAR_FLAG(dropbehind, FOLIO_HEAD_PAGE)
 	__FOLIO_SET_FLAG(dropbehind, FOLIO_HEAD_PAGE)
 
+/*
+ * A folio with PG_rcu_free set is not returned to the page allocator until
+ * an RCU grace period after its refcount reached zero.  A lockless lookup
+ * that found the folio under rcu_read_lock() can then access its memory
+ * until rcu_read_unlock(), provided the folio was removed from where the
+ * lookup finds it, and the flag set, before its last reference was dropped.
+ * The flag is cleared when the folio is freed.
+ *
+ * Not for folios freed with free_pages_nolock(), or freed somewhere other
+ * than the page allocator (hugetlb).
+ */
+FOLIO_FLAG(rcu_free, FOLIO_HEAD_PAGE)
+	__FOLIO_CLEAR_FLAG(rcu_free, FOLIO_HEAD_PAGE)
+
 #ifdef CONFIG_HIGHMEM
 /*
  * Must use a macro here due to header dependency issues. page_zone() is not
diff --git a/include/trace/events/mmflags.h b/include/trace/events/mmflags.h
index 935893e5e..87d00f431 100644
--- a/include/trace/events/mmflags.h
+++ b/include/trace/events/mmflags.h
@@ -151,7 +151,8 @@ TRACE_DEFINE_ENUM(___GFP_LAST_BIT);
 	DEF_PAGEFLAG_NAME(reclaim),					\
 	DEF_PAGEFLAG_NAME(swapbacked),					\
 	DEF_PAGEFLAG_NAME(unevictable),					\
-	DEF_PAGEFLAG_NAME(dropbehind)					\
+	DEF_PAGEFLAG_NAME(dropbehind),					\
+	DEF_PAGEFLAG_NAME(rcu_free)					\
 IF_HAVE_PG_MLOCK(mlocked)						\
 IF_HAVE_PG_HWPOISON(hwpoison)						\
 IF_HAVE_PG_IDLE(idle)							\
diff --git a/mm/huge_memory.c b/mm/huge_memory.c
index 1e5d68acf..c1c8125c4 100644
--- a/mm/huge_memory.c
+++ b/mm/huge_memory.c
@@ -3743,6 +3743,7 @@ static void __split_folio_to_order(struct folio *folio, int old_order,
 #endif
 				 (1L << PG_dirty) |
 				 (1L << PG_dropbehind) |
+				 (1L << PG_rcu_free) |
 				 LRU_GEN_MASK | LRU_REFS_MASK));
 
 		new_folio->mapping = folio->mapping;
diff --git a/mm/page_alloc.c b/mm/page_alloc.c
index 12fac9084..44eba1f67 100644
--- a/mm/page_alloc.c
+++ b/mm/page_alloc.c
@@ -1313,6 +1313,111 @@ static inline void pgalloc_tag_sub_pages(struct alloc_tag *tag, unsigned int nr)
 
 #endif /* CONFIG_MEM_ALLOC_PROFILING */
 
+/*
+ * Folios with PG_rcu_free set wait here for an RCU grace period before
+ * they go back to the allocator.  They are linked through ->lru: a folio
+ * whose refcount has reached zero is on no LRU and not yet on the
+ * allocator's lists.
+ *
+ * Each CPU has at most one grace period in flight.  @batch holds the
+ * folios it covers: they were moved from @pending when queue_rcu_work()
+ * started it.  Folios freed meanwhile collect on @pending.  When the grace
+ * period ends, the work takes @batch, moves @pending to @batch and starts
+ * the next grace period, and then frees what it took.  The move and the
+ * queue_rcu_work() happen together under @lock, so no folio joins a batch
+ * after its grace period started.  A folio waits one to two grace periods.
+ *
+ * @batch is non-empty from the queue_rcu_work() until the work has taken
+ * it.  So @batch being empty means that no grace period is in flight and
+ * @rwork is not queued.
+ *
+ * Reclaim makes progress only when these frees complete, so the work must
+ * not wait for a new worker: the workqueue is WQ_MEM_RECLAIM.
+ *
+ * rcu_free_init() runs before vfs_caches_init(), so before anything can
+ * be mounted: no folio is in a page cache until the workqueue exists.
+ */
+struct rcu_free_pcpu {
+	spinlock_t lock;
+	struct list_head pending;
+	struct list_head batch;
+	struct rcu_work rwork;
+};
+static DEFINE_PER_CPU(struct rcu_free_pcpu, rcu_free_pcpu) = {
+	.lock = __SPIN_LOCK_UNLOCKED(rcu_free_pcpu.lock),
+};
+static struct workqueue_struct *rcu_free_wq __ro_after_init;
+
+/* Called with r->lock held */
+static void rcu_free_start(struct rcu_free_pcpu *r)
+{
+	/* A grace period is in flight: its work starts the next one */
+	if (!list_empty(&r->batch))
+		return;
+
+	list_splice_init(&r->pending, &r->batch);
+	if (!list_empty(&r->batch))
+		WARN_ON_ONCE(!queue_rcu_work(rcu_free_wq, &r->rwork));
+}
+
+static void rcu_free_pages(struct list_head *list)
+{
+	struct folio_batch fbatch;
+	struct folio *folio, *next;
+
+	folio_batch_init(&fbatch);
+	/* Freeing a folio reuses its ->lru: @next is read before that */
+	list_for_each_entry_safe(folio, next, list, lru) {
+		if (!folio_batch_add(&fbatch, folio)) {
+			free_unref_folios(&fbatch);
+			/* Unbounded: one truncate can put a whole file here */
+			cond_resched();
+		}
+	}
+	if (folio_batch_count(&fbatch))
+		free_unref_folios(&fbatch);
+}
+
+static void rcu_free_workfn(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct rcu_free_pcpu *r = container_of(to_rcu_work(work),
+					       struct rcu_free_pcpu, rwork);
+	LIST_HEAD(done);
+
+	spin_lock_irq(&r->lock);
+	list_splice_init(&r->batch, &done);
+	rcu_free_start(r);
+	spin_unlock_irq(&r->lock);
+
+	rcu_free_pages(&done);
+}
+
+static void rcu_free_defer(struct folio *folio)
+{
+	struct rcu_free_pcpu *r = raw_cpu_ptr(&rcu_free_pcpu);
+	unsigned long flags;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&r->lock, flags);
+	list_add_tail(&folio->lru, &r->pending);
+	rcu_free_start(r);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&r->lock, flags);
+}
+
+static void __init rcu_free_init(void)
+{
+	int cpu;
+
+	rcu_free_wq = alloc_workqueue("rcu_free", WQ_MEM_RECLAIM | WQ_PERCPU, 0);
+
+	for_each_possible_cpu(cpu) {
+		struct rcu_free_pcpu *r = per_cpu_ptr(&rcu_free_pcpu, cpu);
+
+		INIT_LIST_HEAD(&r->pending);
+		INIT_LIST_HEAD(&r->batch);
+		INIT_RCU_WORK(&r->rwork, rcu_free_workfn);
+	}
+}
+
 static __always_inline bool __free_pages_prepare(struct page *page,
 		unsigned int order, fpi_t fpi_flags)
 {
@@ -1327,6 +1432,25 @@ static __always_inline bool __free_pages_prepare(struct page *page,
 
 	VM_BUG_ON_PAGE(PageTail(page), page);
 
+	/*
+	 * Defer: rcu_free_pages() frees the folio after a grace period, and
+	 * it comes through here again with the flag clear.  rcu_free_defer()
+	 * takes a spinlock, so the flag must not be set on a folio freed
+	 * with free_pages_nolock() (FPI_NOLOCK).
+	 *
+	 * Not for a hwpoisoned order-0 page: it never reaches the allocator
+	 * (see below), and page_handle_poison() takes a reference on it right
+	 * after the put that brought it here, so rcu_free_pages() would free
+	 * a page that has a reference.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(folio_test_rcu_free(folio)) &&
+	    !(PageHWPoison(page) && !order)) {
+		VM_WARN_ON_ONCE(fpi_flags & FPI_NOLOCK);
+		__folio_clear_rcu_free(folio);
+		rcu_free_defer(folio);
+		return false;
+	}
+
 	trace_mm_page_free(page, order);
 	kmsan_free_page(page, order);
 
@@ -6401,6 +6525,8 @@ void __init setup_per_cpu_pageset(void)
 	for_each_online_pgdat(pgdat)
 		pgdat->per_cpu_nodestats =
 			alloc_percpu(struct per_cpu_nodestat);
+
+	rcu_free_init();
 }
 
 __meminit void zone_pcp_init(struct zone *zone)

From 4ecdb93a37c4a651f04f98996332e0c413b1e302 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Linus Torvalds <torvalds@linux-foundation.org>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 02:23:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/9] mm: free folios removed from the page cache only after an
 RCU grace period

Set PG_rcu_free on a folio when it is taken out of a mapping's i_pages
("mark" it).  A lockless page cache lookup that loaded a folio pointer
under rcu_read_lock() then cannot find that folio's memory reallocated
before rcu_read_unlock().  It can still find the refcount frozen by
reclaim, migration or split, so folio_try_get() stays conditional.  The
lockless read later in this series depends on this.

folio_mark_removed_from_cache() is called where a folio leaves i_pages,
before the page cache's references to it are dropped:
page_cache_delete() (so every __filemap_remove_folio(): truncate,
reclaim, invalidate, split tails past EOF), page_cache_delete_batch(),
replace_page_cache_folio(), __folio_migrate_mapping(), collapse_file()
and shmem_delete_from_page_cache().  The list comes from a git grep of
the xarray store and erase calls in mm/ and fs/.  nilfs2 moves folios
between its own internal mappings without freeing them, and is left
alone.

Not covered: hugetlb folios, which go back to the hugetlb pool, and the
swap cache.  A shmem folio that moves to the swap cache is marked when it
leaves the shmem mapping.

The flag only delays the free.  A folio that is removed and then given
another use without being freed is not delayed.

From here on, memory waits on the lists of the previous patch.  Those
folios have refcount 0, are not PageBuddy and are not in NR_FREE_PAGES,
and nothing else in mm/ knows about them:

 - alloc_contig_range() migrates page cache out of a range and then
   finds the source folios still busy in test_pages_isolated(), so it
   returns -EBUSY.
 - should_reclaim_retry() does not count them as available, so it can
   decide for the OOM killer while reclaimed page cache is still
   waiting; reclaim overshoots for the same reason.
 - Compaction does not find the pages it migrated away from on the free
   lists.
 - Memory offlining loops until they reach the free lists.

The lists need a drain where drain_all_pages() is called, and a counter
that the allocator can read.  Neither is in this series.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Linus Torvalds <torvalds@linux-foundation.org>
