From 74932d065294b1804e4d152f759ae054e6f3bb49 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Linus Torvalds <torvalds@linux-foundation.org>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 05:56:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/9] mm: don't defer the free of page cache folios of an inode
 being evicted

Deleting a large file sends all of its page cache through the deferred
free: a lock and a list for every folio, and one to two grace periods
before the memory is free, for folios that no lookup can reach any more.

This patch adds the rule that makes that last part true: a lockless
lookup that relies on PG_rcu_free has to hold a reference to the inode
that owns the mapping.  I_FREEING is set only once the inode has no
references (fs/inode.c), so a folio removed from the mapping of an inode
with I_FREEING set cannot be in use by such a lookup.  Don't set
PG_rcu_free on it.  The rule is stated in the lockless protocol comment
and at folio_mark_removed_from_cache(), which now takes the mapping.

A lookup on behalf of an open file follows the rule if the file holds
the inode that owns file->f_mapping.  From a git grep of the assignments
to ->f_mapping:

 - the common case: f_mapping is the mapping of the file's own inode,
   which the file's dentry holds;
 - block devices: the bdev inode's mapping.  The file holds bd_device,
   and the bdev inode's own reference is dropped only in part_release()
   and disk_release();
 - coda: the host file's mapping.  The coda file keeps host_file open;
 - alloc_file_clone(): the original file's mapping and its struct path,
   so the same dentry;
 - device files (drm, vfio, dax, /dev/mem, PCI resources), which do not
   read through the page cache; checked by name, not by reading each
   ->read_iter().

erofs page cache sharing reads through a second file, opened on the
shared inode, and passes that file to filemap_read(), which is the
common case again.

AS_EXITING is not the same test: f2fs calls truncate_inode_pages_final()
on inodes that are open, from f2fs_ioc_start_atomic_write() and
f2fs_abort_atomic_write(), and nothing clears AS_EXITING again.

A remover that sees I_FREEING skips the mark, so no lookup may still be
inside its RCU read section at that point.  The remover need not be the
evicting task.  Reclaim, truncation and invalidation test I_FREEING with
mapping->host->i_lock held, and it is set under i_lock.
replace_page_cache_folio(), __folio_migrate_mapping() and
shmem_delete_from_page_cache() hold only the i_pages lock, and
collapse_file() only the folio lock.  For those the argument is one of
ordering: a lookup's last access to the folio comes before the last
reference to its file is dropped, that comes before the last reference
to the inode is dropped, and I_FREEING is set after that.  The file's
and the inode's reference drops are value-returning atomic operations,
which are fully ordered (Documentation/atomic_t.txt); the dentry step
between them was not looked at separately.  None of this has been
checked with a tools/memory-model litmus test.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Linus Torvalds <torvalds@linux-foundation.org>
